[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253421 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fbf6248-d12e-5d93-9bd6-a758219afffd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-\\frac{4}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250822} {:answer/id #uuid "5e600195-fcd8-538b-a937-d7235c93759b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=\\frac{8}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251332} {:answer/id #uuid "7fe30735-910b-53cf-8315-28f2b7b166dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-\\frac{2}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251629} {:answer/id #uuid "c65311d7-4cfa-5614-9150-f67f2333bec4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-\\frac{8}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252305} {:answer/id #uuid "c9b5a3e2-e956-57f9-a0b3-988ac49b54dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=\\frac{4}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252338}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c65311d7-4cfa-5614-9150-f67f2333bec4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-\\frac{8}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252305} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd192c1c-2288-5933-9db5-d4953ee4f919" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253183}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "15283c46-c4af-5a49-a06f-7a6077e8f4df" :question/math-academy-id "q-11782" :question/problem "Find the equation of the downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and which passes through the point $(4,-6).$" :question/worked-solution "We know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola whose vertex is $(0,0),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe're told that the given parabola passes through the point $(4,-6),$ so we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=-6$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {4}^{2} & =4p(-6) \\\\ 16 & =-24p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4(-\\frac{2}{3})y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-\\frac{8}{3}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253426 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08bb1537-d88e-5b3f-911a-ebda185d71d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250631} {:answer/id #uuid "154d1a5b-94de-5332-826d-f7085ae1bbac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250735} {:answer/id #uuid "6982de92-482e-5773-8de9-08b335a18349" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251422} {:answer/id #uuid "8612ead4-47dc-5874-b6e3-9dfd76a3c1d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251675} {:answer/id #uuid "ad9bf3b6-8f0d-5e32-8900-ae60d369c4d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252066}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "154d1a5b-94de-5332-826d-f7085ae1bbac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250735} :answer-field/id #uuid "5303117c-9cc9-5aa9-a8ac-5b23f63b8352" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252952}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3e0561fe-63de-5941-8751-b8579e1f5296" :question/math-academy-id "q-117890" :question/problem "![](images/d5/d54c590d8d64670c0fb90baea864d154cb4c59cd69d7f57234bfd8a5c4a3bfe7.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$" :question/worked-solution "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{4}(x+4C))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+4C$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{4}(x+4C))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/33/336c38a44a3a40fcaf4a226f61a9f7af2cc731674c1ccc6679b80d846d4ea657.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})$ by $0-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ units to the left. Therefore\n\n$$\n4C=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{\\pi }{8}.\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ is in this interval, the value of $C$ is $\\frac{\\pi }{8}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253428 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ea779ad-ec48-521d-9873-3e1098609beb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=6y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250681} {:answer/id #uuid "26fb3e3f-770f-5df6-923e-38963503ea83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-9y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250888} {:answer/id #uuid "48e77783-9ad9-5e52-89cc-6011483b0bf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=3y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251162} {:answer/id #uuid "4c6c7a4b-1086-57f7-8232-17769fe13155" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-6y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251188} {:answer/id #uuid "d3868abe-7ed1-513e-9f3c-2370b863a82e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=9y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252426}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3868abe-7ed1-513e-9f3c-2370b863a82e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=9y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252426} :answer-field/id #uuid "72fca4e8-e475-5995-aea2-dfc3819c73d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253009}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cd21d19c-e94c-54e2-a9f4-0d018bf794c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-11790" :question/problem "Find the equation of the upward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and which passes through the point $(6,4).$" :question/worked-solution "We know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola whose vertex is $(0,0),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe're told that the given parabola passes through the point $(6,4),$ so we can substitute $x=6$ and $y=4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {6}^{2} & =4p(4) \\\\ 36 & =16p \\\\ p & =\\frac{9}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4(\\frac{9}{4})y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =9y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253429 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1697c13e-207a-51d1-8cdb-ea9879f32fac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=5y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250748} {:answer/id #uuid "8eaf8ea3-a21f-5f6c-a1e2-8ebd403498f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251764} {:answer/id #uuid "9a8fbf3a-23f5-5d9d-b6db-22f9b7f20816" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=3y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251880} {:answer/id #uuid "a47ed364-cece-5bcd-a59e-af9a0ba7aa53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-5y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251973} {:answer/id #uuid "eafb55c5-ac74-5bab-a5a8-a35fdbb3d528" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-3y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252611}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a47ed364-cece-5bcd-a59e-af9a0ba7aa53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-5y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251973} :answer-field/id #uuid "46af8ee6-d997-5905-9eaa-b9d380170b0f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252928}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "27084a71-9ed1-579f-859f-319625b653b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-11791" :question/problem "![](images/48/48ae8debc8c92587e19b6c1cb67e426ef2fcf8a91d75a694a117c5e649cbd3f8.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the parabola is downward-opening, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(-5,-5).$\n\nFor any upward or downward-opening parabola whose vertex is $(0,0),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe know that the given parabola passes through the point $(-5,-5),$ so we can substitute $x=-5$ and $y=-5$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ (-5)^{2} & =4p(-5) \\\\ 25 & =-20p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4(-\\frac{5}{4})y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-5y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253430 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29bfd946-372a-5444-a539-b716d9cbb951" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=5y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250911} {:answer/id #uuid "315d3443-669e-53c8-825c-4dd3c15813b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250972} {:answer/id #uuid "7a6adb72-8b7c-5af0-8717-9d8ed3666c4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-3y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251572} {:answer/id #uuid "96e517f3-26bc-5e99-8b69-bc21a87de470" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-5y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251847} {:answer/id #uuid "97998a1b-9445-5245-b410-a419f925511e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=3y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251855}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29bfd946-372a-5444-a539-b716d9cbb951" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=5y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250911} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d6c6253-24f7-571b-b3ec-a0a27c5878db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252942}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e0b80741-e06b-5c43-b887-c4210becc6d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-11792" :question/problem "Find the equation of the upward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and which passes through the point $(-5,5).$" :question/worked-solution "We know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola whose vertex is $(0,0),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe're told that the given parabola passes through the point $(-5,5),$ so we can substitute $x=-5$ and $y=5$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ (-5)^{2} & =4p(5) \\\\ 25 & =20p \\\\ p & =\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4(\\frac{5}{4})y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =5y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253440 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "460b29d8-8c41-5819-a7bc-f57a1ada08e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+4)^{2}=-2(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251133} {:answer/id #uuid "7f9b7b7e-cd07-5b14-823d-05a02712ce26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+4)^{2}=-(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251627} {:answer/id #uuid "86b60e74-7815-5d60-83e1-d092fc95136b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+4)^{2}=2(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251683} {:answer/id #uuid "902f3173-2e96-592c-8a0a-589113044ffd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+4)^{2}=(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251776} {:answer/id #uuid "f7248341-088d-565f-8957-1786ff472c31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-4)^{2}=-(x+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252713}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f9b7b7e-cd07-5b14-823d-05a02712ce26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+4)^{2}=-(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251627} :answer-field/id #uuid "180a0936-8a8a-5f94-ae2b-bfa0aa9cf17b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252849}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "36fb798c-df79-55a4-8a2e-364abb45d1ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-11799" :question/problem "![](images/87/872c4385f2f7075ac4f9690f5adabaa8cb6b4ad3ffe738b47f2b22e477aab621.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the left. The parabola has its vertex at $(4,-4)$ and passes through the point $(0,-6).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(4,-4),$ we substitute $h=4$ and $k=-4$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+4)^{2} & =4p(x-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(0,-6),$ so we can substitute $x=0$ and $y=-6$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+4)^{2} & =4p(x-4) \\\\ (-6+4)^{2} & =4p(0-4) \\\\ 4 & =-16p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+4)^{2} & =4p(x-4) \\\\ (y+4)^{2} & =4(-\\frac{1}{4})(x-4) \\\\ (y+4)^{2} & =-(x-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the left since $p=-\\frac{1}{4}$ is negative."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253441 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f04379b-3d65-5fe7-a01c-56d245e87cdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+4)^{2}=x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250817} {:answer/id #uuid "5ac3aca2-1c63-574b-a675-0fcc8209b55a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+1)^{2}=2(x+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251302} {:answer/id #uuid "98d79e4d-a781-5885-bfe3-739c2440f290" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+1)^{2}=-2(x+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251866} {:answer/id #uuid "bdd22a38-8f89-5939-b74d-0e8d05e5bf6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+1)^{2}=x+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252232} {:answer/id #uuid "befca774-3fa1-5095-954a-dc3b54124088" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+1)^{2}=-(x+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bdd22a38-8f89-5939-b74d-0e8d05e5bf6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+1)^{2}=x+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252232} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ac5191f-267a-5a9c-b564-e5f87736e72c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252903}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "276d74cc-81ab-53f8-848e-d3cdf1f3ff74" :question/math-academy-id "q-11800" :question/problem "Find the equation of the right-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(-4,-1)$ and passes through the point $(5,2).$" :question/worked-solution "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(-4,-1),$ we substitute $h=-4$ and $k=-1$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =4p(x+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(5,2),$ so we can substitute $x=5$ and $y=2$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =4p(x+4) \\\\ (2+1)^{2} & =4p(5+4) \\\\ 9 & =36p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =4p(x+4) \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{4})(x+4) \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =x+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253442 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0178a51e-ad3f-580a-8178-1545ec37e1de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-2)^{2}=-\\frac{8}{3}(x+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250565} {:answer/id #uuid "04f7e811-64c1-5c0c-9fa9-a4fd1945c33d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-2)^{2}=\\frac{8}{3}(x+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250590} {:answer/id #uuid "38b4bf09-bdf2-55e5-bbec-4ef5084cfca3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-2)^{2}=-\\frac{16}{3}(x+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251024} {:answer/id #uuid "c2e7ad24-f5ea-5748-be42-83f7da6fbde0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+4)^{2}=\\frac{8}{3}(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252275} {:answer/id #uuid "f1ff1551-4a70-5775-b0b2-938dc974d575" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-2)^{2}=\\frac{16}{3}(x+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252669}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04f7e811-64c1-5c0c-9fa9-a4fd1945c33d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-2)^{2}=\\frac{8}{3}(x+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250590} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cdd124b-7372-5e02-b942-7c2d5ff6a119" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252858}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9d9f39bd-2803-56ae-905c-30bd79159a52" :question/math-academy-id "q-11801" :question/problem "![](images/d4/d4d0ad25fe8409b55ad4021c5e06d0f13579cea804b1a2b78b18b3ba76bd0c0e.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the right. The parabola has its vertex at $(-4,2)$ and passes through the point $(2,6).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(-4,2),$ we substitute $h=-4$ and $k=2$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =4p(x+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(2,6),$ so we can substitute $x=2$ and $y=6$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =4p(x+4) \\\\ (6-2)^{2} & =4p(2+4) \\\\ 16 & =24p \\\\ p & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =4p(x+4) \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =4(\\frac{2}{3})(x+4) \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =\\frac{8}{3}(x+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the right since $p=\\frac{2}{3}$ is positive."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253443 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00eabfa3-cbd8-5d06-b474-2130cea60e32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-1)^{2}=\\frac{4}{3}(x-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250558} {:answer/id #uuid "2b0a6616-b04e-5277-9ba6-2b86840c5e11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-1)^{2}=-\\frac{8}{3}(x-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250920} {:answer/id #uuid "5ae9419c-4622-5326-9759-7bf6e6f53229" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-1)^{2}=-\\frac{4}{3}(x-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251304} {:answer/id #uuid "5bebc376-58df-5442-bba6-b167bd6045ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-1)^{2}=\\frac{8}{3}(x-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251311} {:answer/id #uuid "7ec8aee3-46a8-5db4-a234-1e0983fb9ed7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-5)^{2}=-\\frac{8}{3}(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251616}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b0a6616-b04e-5277-9ba6-2b86840c5e11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-1)^{2}=-\\frac{8}{3}(x-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250920} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfb9c786-7275-567d-8803-668e5cc54126" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253186}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b010d0fe-74c1-571a-98b5-1a828c1293c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-11802" :question/problem "Find the equation of the left-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(5,1)$ and passes through the point $(-1,5).$" :question/worked-solution "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(5,1),$ we substitute $h=5$ and $k=1$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-1)^{2} & =4p(x-5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(-1,5),$ so we can substitute $x=-1$ and $y=5$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-1)^{2} & =4p(x-5) \\\\ (5-1)^{2} & =4p(-1-5) \\\\ 16 & =-24p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-1)^{2} & =4p(x-5) \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =4(-\\frac{2}{3})(x-5) \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =-\\frac{8}{3}(x-5).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253445 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4018a4c1-4153-5b07-852b-733bcee2f7b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-2)^{2}=6(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251083} {:answer/id #uuid "6b9f1cb9-c74a-55b9-960d-d70871d68d17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-2)^{2}=-4(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251434} {:answer/id #uuid "72ee0d53-5290-5adb-b783-4d145143bd60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-2)^{2}=4(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251500} {:answer/id #uuid "a41d3d7d-5ed4-5a2c-a7f6-5269a11e675a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-2)^{2}=-6(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251970} {:answer/id #uuid "dc38ee73-e991-5df6-93e5-994939fafb0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+2)^{2}=-6(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252492}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a41d3d7d-5ed4-5a2c-a7f6-5269a11e675a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-2)^{2}=-6(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251970} :answer-field/id #uuid "0aad245d-2740-5761-a7f2-fd3ae17120cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252828}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f478aeca-2c7f-5ae7-9dbf-a602b464edd0" :question/math-academy-id "q-11803" :question/problem "![](images/81/814515b0294849e2689214faa1ac97a4f0bb5134f3209a9fb0b2fdbda5c2a484.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the left. The parabola has its vertex at $(2,2)$ and passes through the point $(-4,8).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(2,2),$ we substitute $h=2$ and $k=2$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =4p(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(-4,8),$ so we can substitute $x=-4$ and $y=8$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =4p(x-2) \\\\ (8-2)^{2} & =4p(-4-2) \\\\ 36 & =-24p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =4p(x-2) \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =4(-\\frac{3}{2})(x-2) \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =-6(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the left since $p=-\\frac{3}{2}$ is negative."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253446 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d8acb62-1689-516f-9882-4c9dda55e1c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=2(x+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251328} {:answer/id #uuid "77de82a0-e73e-5de8-a117-54e53271cdb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-3(x+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251544} {:answer/id #uuid "7a6921a9-f376-5914-8a9e-5b2f42ba5a37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+4)^{2}=3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251571} {:answer/id #uuid "95781b19-83eb-5875-9326-f078ecb9c39d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=3(x+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251837} {:answer/id #uuid "fc5538d8-0f12-5dc5-a2c9-99178bd82c8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-2(x+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252764}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95781b19-83eb-5875-9326-f078ecb9c39d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=3(x+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251837} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bf11644-eb3c-55f3-8b1b-a01c321a67d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253078}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "157ae011-ca8e-518d-b6fc-d2c2017281bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-11805" :question/problem "![](images/a1/a16f3f2ba78b5aef180c4331d1dd99f37340d2c6d842c1596e35445f1dbf34c5.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the right. The parabola has its vertex at $(-4,0)$ and passes through the point $(8,6).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(-4,0),$ we substitute $h=-4$ and $k=0$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4p(x+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(8,6),$ so we can substitute $x=8$ and $y=6$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4p(x+4) \\\\ {6}^{2} & =4p(8+4) \\\\ 36 & =48p \\\\ p & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4p(x+4) \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(\\frac{3}{4})(x+4) \\\\ {y}^{2} & =3(x+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the right since $p=\\frac{3}{4}$ is positive."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253447 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29aa3cd1-7799-5135-8c80-c4be163b9016" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-4)^{2}=-3(x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250910} {:answer/id #uuid "439b2927-b0e0-5173-a8a9-6f34626fa295" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+3)^{2}=2(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251119} {:answer/id #uuid "a9745f1a-6679-5e5d-a37d-7e8e72bd55cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+3)^{2}=-2(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252019} {:answer/id #uuid "bd53c992-2321-5abf-998f-e25533a8ba8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+3)^{2}=3(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252227} {:answer/id #uuid "f49db360-9feb-52fe-afbf-23affd8d48ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+3)^{2}=-3(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252695}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f49db360-9feb-52fe-afbf-23affd8d48ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+3)^{2}=-3(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252695} :answer-field/id #uuid "052f3cd6-876b-5768-809f-fb421d07e2fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252818}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aa15fb81-7036-5f0a-83c0-c74afd28471c" :question/math-academy-id "q-11806" :question/problem "Find the equation of the left-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(4,-3)$ and passes through the point $(1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(4,-3),$ we substitute $h=4$ and $k=-3$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+3)^{2} & =4p(x-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(1,0),$ so we can substitute $x=1$ and $y=0$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+3)^{2} & =4p(x-4) \\\\ (0+3)^{2} & =4p(1-4) \\\\ 9 & =-12p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+3)^{2} & =4p(x-4) \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =4(-\\frac{3}{4})(x-4) \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =-3(x-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253448 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01377482-fc4a-5589-9987-6e11e8378009" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-2)^{2}=\\frac{16}{3}(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250563} {:answer/id #uuid "2f55068e-d36b-5d90-8356-de9905dd183d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-2)^{2}=\\frac{8}{3}(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250956} {:answer/id #uuid "977f2f6a-4477-554a-b7a0-8b06a558860f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-2)^{2}=-\\frac{16}{3}(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251852} {:answer/id #uuid "a35316b4-b511-547c-981f-4edbc9a70443" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-2)^{2}=-\\frac{8}{3}(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251961} {:answer/id #uuid "ccfb8090-5046-5651-93f5-bb4b9e5b1002" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-4)^{2}=-\\frac{16}{3}(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252360}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "977f2f6a-4477-554a-b7a0-8b06a558860f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-2)^{2}=-\\frac{16}{3}(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251852} :answer-field/id #uuid "64de54a2-95fe-5b7e-8041-becf021848fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252987}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bfe2de45-9f13-54bf-92ae-4e435ecb2b1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-11807" :question/problem "![](images/e4/e4b681e9bdf5fa8021ca359e0bf93c27b53a18f0d5e906765a911a72ecfc0159.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the left. The parabola has its vertex at $(4,2)$ and passes through the point $(-8,10).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(4,2),$ we substitute $h=4$ and $k=2$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =4p(x-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(-8,10),$ so we can substitute $x=-8$ and $y=10$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =4p(x-4) \\\\ (10-2)^{2} & =4p(-8-4) \\\\ 64 & =-48p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =4p(x-4) \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =4(-\\frac{4}{3})(x-4) \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =-\\frac{16}{3}(x-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the left since $p=-\\frac{4}{3}$ is negative."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253450 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7fb95a60-1161-5413-93d5-4b228c414c07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-1)^{2}=\\frac{8}{3}(x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251628} {:answer/id #uuid "8826e546-c952-5f6f-a2bf-20e6c02687db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-1)^{2}=-\\frac{8}{3}(x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251699} {:answer/id #uuid "93c275c0-0d07-5428-b0c7-6e0602380964" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-1)^{2}=\\frac{16}{3}(x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251823} {:answer/id #uuid "982e9f2d-6c4e-53e4-ab0f-8177505b24b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+3)^{2}=\\frac{16}{3}(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251861} {:answer/id #uuid "f71d1909-9157-5190-b146-6762b6207b86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-1)^{2}=-\\frac{16}{3}(x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252711}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93c275c0-0d07-5428-b0c7-6e0602380964" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-1)^{2}=\\frac{16}{3}(x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251823} :answer-field/id #uuid "99f96282-dac8-5d52-9ca5-9060d3ea0642" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253074}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c384e88f-5df3-50d8-843d-d7ee59f7b038" :question/math-academy-id "q-11808" :question/problem "Find the equation of the right-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(-3,1)$ and passes through the point $(0,5).$" :question/worked-solution "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(-3,1),$ we substitute $h=-3$ and $k=1$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-1)^{2} & =4p(x+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(0,5),$ so we can substitute $x=0$ and $y=5$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-1)^{2} & =4p(x+3) \\\\ (5-1)^{2} & =4p(0+3) \\\\ 16 & =12p \\\\ p & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-1)^{2} & =4p(x+3) \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =4(\\frac{4}{3})(x+3) \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =\\frac{16}{3}(x+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253452 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "531adca8-60bb-50a4-8900-a46ffe6dc0af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+6)^{2}=9x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251231} {:answer/id #uuid "6f0a7535-d9b3-5d12-a834-77b7f828e0a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=9(x+6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251458} {:answer/id #uuid "8b12f5d5-879c-51b4-b347-73615cacbb41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-6(x+6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251728} {:answer/id #uuid "abda7147-8065-57e1-81d4-1ed5f2cb4f24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-9(x+6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252043} {:answer/id #uuid "c16cc020-1c53-5f88-9dce-79606ba3537c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=6(x+6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f0a7535-d9b3-5d12-a834-77b7f828e0a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=9(x+6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251458} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2aeb479-547c-5991-8f8f-b20a9eb96bd7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253194}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "582e0262-2ff0-53cf-8f18-89d0545d5f0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-11809" :question/problem "![](images/8a/8a48fcacc437d1dca41df82e24984507a92d686f80f4517c6682e8b14b694f0e.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the right. The parabola has its vertex at $(-6,0)$ and passes through the point $(-2,6).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(-6,0),$ we substitute $h=-6$ and $k=0$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4p(x+6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(-2,6),$ so we can substitute $x=-2$ and $y=6$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4p(x+6) \\\\ {6}^{2} & =4p(-2+6) \\\\ 36 & =16p \\\\ p & =\\frac{9}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4p(x+6) \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(\\frac{9}{4})(x+6) \\\\ {y}^{2} & =9(x+6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the right since $p=\\frac{9}{4}$ is positive."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253454 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08b7a1e2-2234-5972-a128-08fb39f06dbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-4)^{2}=5(x-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250630} {:answer/id #uuid "b60bb7a8-52f6-584f-a4bf-3e844e94e661" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-4)^{2}=3(x-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252154} {:answer/id #uuid "c52f456e-d64e-5f46-a9e4-84edfe0766c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-6)^{2}=-5(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252299} {:answer/id #uuid "d7e3e02b-032b-5e7e-9f66-142cdfdd6a61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-4)^{2}=-3(x-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252466} {:answer/id #uuid "fb3d962a-57d2-545b-a36a-897c8ba80fcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-4)^{2}=-5(x-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252756}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb3d962a-57d2-545b-a36a-897c8ba80fcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-4)^{2}=-5(x-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252756} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f980315-6664-5b6c-9a9a-9c681494e4b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252914}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fa256900-e0ea-52f1-985a-4c3b8bdded9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-11811" :question/problem "![](images/ec/ecf128d341a8a568ade4abaa2a59e1083d2c436fdb3d2a0b4b642fb7e85cee63.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the left. The parabola has its vertex at $(6,4)$ and passes through the point $(1,9).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(6,4),$ we substitute $h=6$ and $k=4$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-4)^{2} & =4p(x-6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(1,9),$ so we can substitute $x=1$ and $y=9$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-4)^{2} & =4p(x-6) \\\\ (9-4)^{2} & =4p(1-6) \\\\ 25 & =-20p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-4)^{2} & =4p(x-6) \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =4(-\\frac{5}{4})(x-6) \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =-5(x-6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the left since $p=-\\frac{5}{4}$ is negative."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253458 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d3096b9-f658-5a5e-a9ee-05a065a40ec2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+3)^{2}=5(x+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250669} {:answer/id #uuid "3b6a4707-c77a-57ee-bf7d-6d66af5d99f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+3)^{2}=-5(x+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251046} {:answer/id #uuid "41a25de4-34c7-58f7-a526-4821b209bb8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+4)^{2}=5(x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251100} {:answer/id #uuid "8d7596d2-dcc4-5089-8930-3276299bf88f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+3)^{2}=2(x+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251749} {:answer/id #uuid "c0392c91-bd09-5f41-835e-33d8a1926611" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+3)^{2}=-2(x+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252251}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d3096b9-f658-5a5e-a9ee-05a065a40ec2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+3)^{2}=5(x+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250669} :answer-field/id #uuid "61d9ebf6-042f-5415-8e18-1294497ab7e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252980}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7d7e628d-39ea-5461-9b3a-d34dcc6784ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-11812" :question/problem "Find the equation of the right-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(-4,-3)$ and passes through the point $(1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(-4,-3),$ we substitute $h=-4$ and $k=-3$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+3)^{2} & =4p(x+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(1,2),$ so we can substitute $x=1$ and $y=2$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+3)^{2} & =4p(x+4) \\\\ (2+3)^{2} & =4p(1+4) \\\\ 25 & =20p \\\\ p & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+3)^{2} & =4p(x+4) \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =4(\\frac{5}{4})(x+4) \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =5(x+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253466 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "169c4d0d-a974-5ab0-ab10-ece44e9bf719" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}=4(y+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250749} {:answer/id #uuid "27b81da8-ffe4-5749-9d5c-518f42d88756" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+4)^{2}=2(y+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250893} {:answer/id #uuid "34c9b005-e169-5610-83db-29fce1448453" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}=2(y+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250995} {:answer/id #uuid "7bee81ac-628e-5cc9-9189-39eb038e571c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}=-2(y+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251585} {:answer/id #uuid "d5bedb93-90d7-53ff-82ed-889cadbd470e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}=-4(y+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252446}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34c9b005-e169-5610-83db-29fce1448453" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}=2(y+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250995} :answer-field/id #uuid "72c319bd-e1d9-5c37-820b-e6a962767d54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253008}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ca3b7f51-a5a6-56e7-8463-29fa6d537419" :question/math-academy-id "q-11822" :question/problem "Find the equation of the upward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(-1,-4)$ and which passes through the point $(1,-2).$" :question/worked-solution "For any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(-1,-4).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-(-1))^{2} & =4p(y-(-4)) \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =4p(y+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(1,-2),$ so we can substitute $x=1$ and $y=-2$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =4p(y+4) \\\\ (1+1)^{2} & =4p(-2+4) \\\\ 4 & =8p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =4p(y+4) \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{2})(y+4) \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =2(y+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253468 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "485d5544-4478-5e1e-ba31-099f0ca42c0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}=-\\frac{4}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251155} {:answer/id #uuid "7790b6ad-09cc-5242-8917-679d80de0769" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-\\frac{4}{3}(y-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251542} {:answer/id #uuid "9894b7ea-d939-55b9-8d35-f86bff1406c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=\\frac{2}{3}(y-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251865} {:answer/id #uuid "9e20bebe-abb0-5e90-9b62-958c377754bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-\\frac{2}{3}(y-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251916} {:answer/id #uuid "c9c0a133-b90a-5777-8af4-3da247ef0ee9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=\\frac{4}{3}(y-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252342}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7790b6ad-09cc-5242-8917-679d80de0769" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-\\frac{4}{3}(y-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251542} :answer-field/id #uuid "184511ac-6f3f-58cc-87c2-9f8975f4268f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252850}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "93e2b546-25a2-5a6e-b5f8-8c32e0bcc85a" :question/math-academy-id "q-11825" :question/problem "Find the equation of the downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,4)$ and which passes through the point $(4,-8).$" :question/worked-solution "For any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(0,4).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-0)^{2} & =4p(y-4) \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4p(y-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(4,-8),$ so we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=-8$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4p(y-4) \\\\ {4}^{2} & =4p(-8-4) \\\\ 16 & =-48p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4p(y-4) \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4(-\\frac{1}{3})(y-4) \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-\\frac{4}{3}(y-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253469 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06cec48d-e68e-5bcc-8270-08fe2e886d9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+5)^{2}=y-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250608} {:answer/id #uuid "8e6cc6fa-f362-599f-ade6-ae329e081e03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}=2(y+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251760} {:answer/id #uuid "bb75a7c2-9da9-57cf-a080-80bcbe9c27dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}=-2(y+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252202} {:answer/id #uuid "c9b6a622-8399-502c-875d-4a9c8d81675b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}=-(y+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252339} {:answer/id #uuid "d07899d6-980a-597d-8721-0d4dbbcd9959" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}=y+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252396}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d07899d6-980a-597d-8721-0d4dbbcd9959" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}=y+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252396} :answer-field/id #uuid "9cb337ec-659a-584a-b940-40e7dd1bcc66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253081}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a291d23e-d654-581b-aa5a-090e3a807669" :question/math-academy-id "q-11827" :question/problem "Find the equation of the upward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(3,-5)$ and which passes through the point $(0,4).$" :question/worked-solution "For any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(3,-5).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =4p(y-(-5)) \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =4p(y+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(0,4),$ so we can substitute $x=0$ and $y=4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =4p(y+5) \\\\ (0-3)^{2} & =4p(4+5) \\\\ 9 & =36p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =4p(y+5) \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{4})(y+5) \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =y+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253470 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21973f60-589a-552d-9d03-86344df35380" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}=\\frac{16}{3}(y+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250839} {:answer/id #uuid "23315b5b-3fb4-5fc6-958e-ed872b0f3632" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}=-\\frac{16}{3}(y+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250854} {:answer/id #uuid "83de494d-232c-5c1b-bfa5-b7578b00134a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}=\\frac{8}{3}(y+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251662} {:answer/id #uuid "87380ce2-5ded-5eac-8c25-73dcbd3a3219" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}=\\frac{8}{3}(y-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251690} {:answer/id #uuid "891bc30f-febc-55dd-bcc9-4d76e29dccc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}=-\\frac{8}{3}(y+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251710}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83de494d-232c-5c1b-bfa5-b7578b00134a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}=\\frac{8}{3}(y+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251662} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb87a37b-0fb6-57d7-bb07-60ea3e82d617" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253128}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "49fe4762-714b-5c6d-b7d9-225a3c1ddebb" :question/math-academy-id "q-11828" :question/problem "![](images/4b/4b30029c03e020e6deacdf6d0728978c5a6d86d88e6bbbf1f8c1a6ec88c278d6.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "We know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(4,-2).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n(x-4)^{2}=4p(y+2).\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(0,4),$ so we can substitute $x=0$ and $y=4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2} & =4p(y+2) \\\\ (0-4)^{2} & =4p(4+2) \\\\ 16 & =24p \\\\ p & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2} & =4p(y+2) \\\\ (x-4)^{2} & =4(\\frac{2}{3})(y+2) \\\\ (x-4)^{2} & =\\frac{8}{3}(y+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253471 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0204d1a4-979b-5e5a-91e8-16da198d96f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}=-7(y+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250572} {:answer/id #uuid "03bd375b-9e1f-597d-b5e9-e0b4bc54bc30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}=7(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250582} {:answer/id #uuid "12e32eca-626a-5a03-9f08-308e2110d81a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}=-3(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250714} {:answer/id #uuid "7de8efb9-9a5f-5339-a2b1-15a8967978ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}=-7(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251610} {:answer/id #uuid "efdf1fa1-0772-50d6-9d54-6cbe5ab65088" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}=3(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252652}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7de8efb9-9a5f-5339-a2b1-15a8967978ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}=-7(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251610} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ee73095-9b6e-56be-abd4-9d4c75590945" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253052}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6128a71e-f744-5ae8-9df3-81f671cec4ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-11833" :question/problem "Find the equation of the downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(1,1)$ and which passes through the point $(-6,-6).$" :question/worked-solution "For any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(1,1).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =4p(y-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(-6,-6),$ so we can substitute $x=-6$ and $y=-6$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =4p(y-1) \\\\ (-6-1)^{2} & =4p(-6-1) \\\\ 49 & =-28p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{7}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =4p(y-1) \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =4(-\\frac{7}{4})(y-1) \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =-7(y-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253472 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86be746b-a100-5c81-b09c-64f69d52354d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-sin⁡(\\frac{7x}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251684} {:answer/id #uuid "a9d165e4-04cb-5b98-939c-960f26cf5112" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{7}{4}-sin⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252020} {:answer/id #uuid "b0256387-7df1-50a5-82ff-d81ca490f07f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{7}{4}sin⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252091} {:answer/id #uuid "b49f046c-976a-5805-b1b1-2b7abb41fb1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-sin⁡x-\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252141} {:answer/id #uuid "d4f0698c-7c2c-5b62-addb-c963a8f40249" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-sin⁡(x+\\frac{7}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252436}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0256387-7df1-50a5-82ff-d81ca490f07f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{7}{4}sin⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252091} :answer-field/id #uuid "deb567f0-6e17-59cc-ad2c-c94c01211c94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253184}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8212d812-45a7-565f-b087-e2f78df1febe" :question/math-academy-id "q-118348" :question/problem "![](images/f2/f2d3945780d6d042ae5fa01905cf7740fca0f5f80307124556a1b4724f691683.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the graph shown above?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/6b/6ba976f250811d1ba3109f4aecbfdb2a4de4ee86300fa867e80e2087aa21601f.png)\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=sin⁡x.$\n\n- Then, reflect it in the $x$-axis to give $y=-sin⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, stretch it parallel to the $y$-axis by a stretch factor of $\\frac{7}{4}$ to give $y=-\\frac{7}{4}sin⁡x.$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the graph of $y=-\\frac{7}{4}sin⁡x.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253473 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a2abbd7-97f8-5e38-941b-3ada6db5798c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+4)^{2}=\\frac{16}{3}(y-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251035} {:answer/id #uuid "8787dc86-a84a-5867-8afa-8d199ba176b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+4)^{2}=-\\frac{8}{3}(y-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251694} {:answer/id #uuid "b0735a9e-9190-50e7-a39d-2d9c6954469a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+4)^{2}=-\\frac{16}{3}(y-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252095} {:answer/id #uuid "bbc062ed-3073-5920-bc31-ce60d55001a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-6)^{2}=-\\frac{16}{3}(y+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252207} {:answer/id #uuid "c2ea3214-bf6f-5b3f